+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Two field workers sometimes edit the same visit independently after comparing field notes, and their edits can arrive days apart at the record store. The store must define what a visit's current record becomes when competing edits arrive, without discarding any submission and without treating arrival order as authority. The answer sets the submission envelope for tablet-sync, the shape of review work for review-export, and which record states may enter the Friday export — both sibling segments design against this contract rather than inventing their own.
+
+The choice turns on the team's tenets: *do not silently discard a submitted edit*, *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **G1 — No silent combination of related edits.** A mechanism that may combine two workers' edits into one record without a person noticing fails this gate. The HLD forbids silently discarding an edit; a merge rule that rewrites a species identification because a cover estimate also changed is a subtler version of the same failure.
+- **G2 — Every submission survives as a stored unit.** A mechanism that reduces or rewrites an original submission before review fails this gate.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Review workload** (major): how much coordinator time a season of ~6,000 visits creates.
+- **Merge-rule maintenance** (major): whether correct operation depends on rules someone must keep current as the visit format evolves.
+- **Explainability of the current record** (moderate, from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*): whether a reader can reconstruct why the current record looks the way it does.
+- **Client and sync complexity** (moderate): what tablets must retain offline and what their submission and retry state machines must carry.
+- **Export readiness** (minor): how many visits reach export without coordinator attention.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit submission names its base state and carries structured changes rather than a complete visit. The server reconciles edits derived from the same base: changes touching disjoint fields combine automatically into a single current record; changes touching the same field overlap and go to coordinator review. Maintained merge rules govern each field kind — scalar overwrite, observer-list union, species-entry matching by species code, and so on. Review needs field-level provenance so coordinators can see which worker contributed which combined value. Unresolved visits hold out of the export; resolved ones enter with their provenance.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- G1: **eliminated.** The merge rules cannot carry scientific relationships the format does not name. Revising a species identification and revising its cover estimate touch disjoint entries under a structural rule set — one changes the species code, the other changes a cover number — yet the cover estimate's meaning depends on the identification it was recorded against. Automatic combination crosses that relationship without a person noticing.
+- G2: passes. Original submissions are retained; combination is a derived representation.
+- Review workload: **strong.** Only overlapping field edits reach review; most unrelated corrections resolve without coordinator attention.
+- Merge-rule maintenance: **weak.** Every new field kind needs a rule, and rules must encode assumptions about which edits can be safely combined — assumptions that drift as survey methodology evolves and are exactly where G1 failures originate.
+- Explainability: **partial.** Provenance records which worker set each field, but the semantics of an auto-combined field are the rule's, not either worker's submitted intent.
+- Client and sync complexity: **weak.** Tablets must retain base snapshots, compute and serialize structured diffs, and handle partial-merge outcomes in their retry state machines.
+- Export readiness: **strong.** Disjoint-edit visits become exportable without review.
+
+**Summary.** Minimizes review by pushing correctness into maintained merge rules, at the cost of structural combination that can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand.
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit is a complete revision naming the revision it was made against. A base-on-head submission extends the visit's revision chain and becomes the new current record. A base-off-head submission is retained in full as a competing revision, the head stays put, and the visit is flagged as having unresolved work. A coordinator reviews competing revisions side by side and adopts one as the head; every competing revision remains stored. Unresolved visits hold out of the export. The server never combines, rewrites, or interprets field edits.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- G1: passes. Competing revisions reach a coordinator whenever they overlap at all; nothing is combined without a person choosing.
+- G2: passes. Each revision is the complete submitted visit, immutable and individually retrievable.
+- Review workload: **weak.** Any two workers editing different fields of the same visit still fork the record and both go to review.
+- Merge-rule maintenance: **strong.** There are no merge rules; correctness lives in a single rule (base vs. head) that does not change as the visit format evolves.
+- Explainability: **strong.** The current record is always exactly one worker's submitted visit; the revision history reconstructs every fork and resolution from the store alone.
+- Client and sync complexity: **strong.** Tablets retain a base revision ID per draft and submit complete payloads — no diff computation, no base snapshots, no partial-merge outcomes.
+- Export readiness: **weak.** Forked visits wait for a coordinator; review volume rises with the rate of independent corrections.
+
+**Summary.** Preserves each worker's submitted visit as a unit and keeps the server logic minimal, at the cost of a larger review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions. The deciding element is G1: field-aware merging eliminated itself, because the failure mode — combining scientifically related edits without noticing — is precisely the risk the project's tenets guard against, and the merge rules needed to avoid it would themselves carry the drifted assumptions that create the risk. Against that, the whole-visit approach's costs are operational rather than correctness-bearing: more coordinator review (absorbed — the team chose a bigger queue over maintained merge rules) and more re-keying for workers whose unrelated edits fork (bounded — a coordinator adopting a revision preserves it whole). The decision turns on the tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit* and on *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*; field-aware merging is not ruled out by any requirement that every record receive manual approval — it is ruled out by what automatic combination does to a record's meaning.
+
+Committing forecloses server-side reconciliation: the record store will never merge, so any future reduction in review volume must come from coordinator tooling or workflow changes, not from store logic. It obligates the sibling segments as follows:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must accommodate: retaining the base revision ID with every local draft; submitting complete visit payloads (no diffs, no snapshots); reading back the unresolved-work flag and competing-revision list from visit retrieval; handling the accepted-as-competing outcome — the worker's edit is stored but not current — and explaining that to the worker; and re-basing a draft whose base revision lost a resolution before resubmitting.
+- **Review-export** must accommodate: a revision-comparison view over a visit's open competing revisions (whole visits, side by side, not field diffs); an adoption-based resolution operation that names the coordinator, the adopted revision, and the contributing submissions; an export eligibility rule that holds unresolved visits out of the CSV; and revision-history retrieval for auditing how a current record came to be.
+- **Both** must treat the record store's revision semantics as the single source; neither may invent merge, priority, or arrival-order rules.
+
+If review volume later proves unsustainable, the reopening path is narrower than the original choice: coordinator tooling (batch adoption of trivially unrelated forks) or a restricted merge scope, decided with the scientific staff — not a general field-aware merge.
